= Phylogenetics =

== Overview ==
'''Phylogenetics''' is the study and inference of evolutionary relationships among organisms, genes, sequences, or other evolving lineages.

Phylogenetic analyses commonly represent inferred relationships as trees or, where reticulation matters, networks. Molecular phylogenetics uses DNA, RNA, or protein data together with explicit models or criteria for evolutionary change.

Distance, parsimony, maximum-likelihood, and Bayesian methods make different assumptions and can yield different results. Alignment, sampling, recombination, horizontal transfer, model misspecification, and long-branch attraction can alter inference.

A phylogenetic tree is an inference with uncertainty, not a direct observation of historical events. Gene trees can differ from species trees because of processes such as incomplete lineage sorting, duplication/loss, and horizontal transfer.

== Boundaries ==
* phylogenetic tree ≠ directly observed history
* sequence similarity ≠ phylogeny
* gene tree ≠ species tree by identity

== Related concepts ==
[[Genetics]], [[Genomics]], [[Biological sequence]], [[Sequence alignment]], [[Probability]], [[Uncertainty]], and [[Scientific model]] provide the evolutionary-inference context.

== Phylogenetic trees as tested hypotheses ==
A phylogenetic tree represents an inference of shared ancestry, not a directly observed genealogy. Its branching topology may differ as new taxa, loci or models are added. Rotating a subtree around a branching point does not change the underlying relationships. Branch lengths have specific meanings only when the model and legend define them; they should not always be read as elapsed time.

== Taxonomy, ecology and lineage history ==
Phylogenetic inference and taxonomic classification serve related but different purposes. A formal taxonomic rank need not map one-to-one onto a historical divergence event. Studies of [[Related to::Ecology|ecological]] adaptation can use evolutionary relationships as context, but similarity of a trait may reflect shared ancestry, convergence or environmental selection. A tree alone does not establish why a species occupies a habitat.

== Sampling and model limitations ==
Sequence choice, alignment assumptions, missing lineages, recombination and horizontal gene transfer can make a simple bifurcating gene tree inadequate. Evidence should specify sequences, taxon selection, inference algorithm, uncertainty support and conditions under which the proposed topology is credible.

